rget variable, and hence can be removed.\ndf.drop(columns=['Was_Vehicle_Left_Hand_Drive?'], inplace=True)\n```end\n\n```python\n# Additional preprocessing transformations\n\n# Step 4: Normalizing Engine_Capacity and Age_of_Driver\ndf['Engine_Capacity_(CC)_norm'] = (df['Engine_Capacity_(CC)'] - df['Engine_Capacity_(CC)'].mean()) / df['Engine_Capacity_(CC)'].std()\ndf['Age_of_Driver_norm'] = (df['Age_of_Driver'] - df['Age_of_Driver'].mean()) / df['Age_of_Driver'].std()\n# Usefulness: Normalization ensures features are scaled properly and avoids dominance of features with larger numeric ranges. Both engine size and driver age are likely correlated with driver behavior (potentially sex of the driver), and normalization preserves their relative differences for the downstream model.\n\n# Step 5: Casualty_Age_Gap\ndf['Casualty_Age_Gap'] = abs(df['Age_of_Driver'] - df['Age_of_Casualty'])\n# Feature description: Absolute difference between driver age and associated casualty age.\n# Usefulness: This feature may highlight younger drivers or older drivers with less cautious driving habits, which could correlate with sex of the driver.\n\n# Step 6: High_Casualty_Vehicles\ndf['High_Casualty_Vehicles'] = np.where(df['Number_of_Casualties'] > df['Number_of_Vehicles'], 1, 0)\n# Feature description: Binary indicator of cases where the number of casualties exceeds the number of vehicles involved in the accident.\n# Usefulness: Certain risky driving behaviors might be associated with the driver's sex. A higher ratio of casualties could align with reckless driving patterns or other risky characteristics of one gender.\n```end"]
