ry indicator of cases where the number of casualties exceeds the number of vehicles involved in the accident.\n# Usefulness: Certain risky driving behaviors might be associated with the driver's sex. A higher ratio of casualties could align with reckless driving patterns or other risky characteristics of one gender.\nend", "Here are three potential next steps for feature engineering to improve predictions for the downstream task of predicting the sex of the driver (Sex_of_Driver):\n\n### Step 1: Compute Driver Casualty Age Ratio\npython\n# Feature name: Driver_Casualty_Age_Ratio\n# Feature description: This feature calculates the ratio of the age of the driver to the age of the casualty in each accident.\n# Usefulness: It provides insight into whether certain age relationships between drivers and casualties correlate with specific genders of drivers. Traditionally, certain demographics might exhibit patterns in age differences during accidents.\ndf['Driver_Casualty_Age_Ratio'] = df['Age_of_Driver'] / df['Age_of_Casualty']\nend\n\n### Step 2: Create Interaction between Speed Risk and Engine Capacity\npython\n# Feature name: Speed_Engine_Interaction\n# Feature description: This feature measures the interaction between Speed_Risk_Feature and normalized Engine_Capacity_(CC). It captures the combined contribution of vehicle speed risk and engine power.\n# Usefulness: Faster vehicles or those with larger engines might exhibit gender-specific trends in terms of the driver's tendency to take risks or cause casualties. Interaction features often enhance modeling performance by capturing non-linear relationships between variables.\ndf['Speed_Engine_Interaction'] = df['Speed_Risk_Feature'] * df['Engine_Capacity_(CC)_norm']\nend\n\n### Step 3: Group and Encode Age Bands\npython\n# Feature name: Age_Band_Target_Encoding\n# Feature description: This feature applies target encoding to the categorical Age_Band_of_Driver_bin based on the average Sex_of_Driver, revealing age-specific variations in driver gender.\n# Usefulness: Age bands can influence gender likelihood due to societal factors, driving habits, or insurance-related distributions. Encoding this feature using target value relationships helps provide a more meaningful representation for predictive tasks.\nage_band_mean = df.groupby('Age_Band_of_Driver_bin')['Sex_of_Driver'].mean()\ndf['Age_Band_Target_Encoding'] = df['Age_Band_of_Driver_bin'].map(age_band_mean)\nend\n\n### Dropping Irrelevant Columns\nHere are some columns that might be dropped for various reasons, such as being redundant, highly correlated with other features, or having little relevance to the prediction task:\n\n#### Example Column Dropping: \npython\n# Explanation why the column 2nd_Road_Number is dropped\n# The ‘2nd_Road_Number’ has a high incidence of zero values and seems unlikely to provide meaningful predictive information about the driver’s gender.\ndf.drop(columns=['2nd_Road_Number'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Casualty_Reference is dropped\n# ‘Casualty_Reference’ is merely an identifier for the casualties and does not contribute meaningful predictive insight into the driver's gender.\ndf.drop(columns=['Casualty_Reference'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n### Key Observations and Improvements:\n1. Driver-Centric Features: Focusing on interactions involving the driver (age, speed, vehicle type) can reveal behavioral and accident trends tied to gender.\n2. Target Encoding: Encoding categorical variables based on their relationship to the target (Sex_of_Driver) is a powerful technique to enhance predictive feature richness.\n3. Removing Noise: Columns with identifiers (e.g., 2nd_Road_Number) or other irrelevant features naturally reduce signal-to-noise ratios and can safely be dropped.\n\nEach step is carefully chosen to enrich the dataset with meaningful insights, reduce redundancy, and simplify downstream model learning complexities."), with improvements -
