2026-05-13 03:04:34,087 - INFO - Selected best state: ("python\n# Creating new features using feature engineering methods\n\n# Step 1: Age_Band_of_Driver_bin\n# Feature description: Group drivers into predefined age bins (e.g., 18-25, 26-35, 36-50, 51-65, etc.).\ndf['Age_Band_of_Driver_bin'] = pd.cut(df['Age_of_Driver'], bins=[0, 17, 25, 35, 50, 65, 100], labels=['Under 18', '18-25', '26-35', '36-50', '51-65', '66+'])\n# Usefulness: Grouping drivers into age bands could help identify relationships between driver age categories and driving behaviors. Younger or older drivers might display different tendencies, which could influence the likelihood of predicting the driver's sex.\n\n# Step 2: Speed_Risk_Feature\n# Feature description: Create a feature combining Vehicle Type and Vehicle Manoeuvre, reflecting a risk profile potentially tied to driver behavior.\ndf['Speed_Risk_Feature'] = df['Vehicle_Type'] * df['Vehicle_Manoeuvre']\n# Usefulness: Vehicle types and maneuvers indicate driving patterns. This combination could help identify specific correlations with driver risk-taking behavior, and potentially the sex of the driver.\n\n# Step 3: Engine_Age_Ratio\n# Feature description: Ratio of Engine_Capacity_(CC) to Age_of_Vehicle.\ndf['Engine_Age_Ratio'] = df['Engine_Capacity_(CC)'] / (df['Age_of_Vehicle'] + 1e-6)  # Adding a small value to avoid division by zero.\n# Usefulness: This feature captures whether more experienced drivers tend to drive vehicles with smaller or larger engine capacity than newer drivers. It might be an indicator that is correlated with the driver's sex.\nend\n\npython\n# Dropping less useful features\n\n# Explanation why the column Longitude and Latitude are dropped\n# While geographic location (Longitude/Latitude) can provide a spatial perspective, given this task is about predicting the driver's sex, these might introduce noise rather than add any meaningful information. \n# The accident's location does not have a clear causative relationship with predicting the sex of the driver.\ndf.drop(columns=['Longitude', 'Latitude'], inplace=True)\n\n# Explanation why the column 'Location_Northing_OSGR' and 'Location_Easting_OSGR' are dropped\n# Similar to the longitude and latitude columns, these could indicate locations but are unlikely to provide useful predictive signals for inferring driver sex.\ndf.drop(columns=['Location_Northing_OSGR', 'Location_Easting_OSGR'], inplace=True)\n\n# Explanation why the column 'Was_Vehicle_Left_Hand_Drive?' is dropped\n# This feature has a highly imbalanced distribution (Mean = 1.0018), which implies the vast majority of vehicles are not left-hand drive. \n# It is likely to have minimal influence on the target variable, and hence can be removed.\ndf.drop(columns=['Was_Vehicle_Left_Hand_Drive?'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Additional preprocessing transformations\n\n# Step 4: Normalizing Engine_Capacity and Age_of_Driver\ndf['Engine_Capacity_(CC)_norm'] = (df['Engine_Capacity_(CC)'] - df['Engine_Capacity_(CC)'].mean()) / df['Engine_Capacity_(CC)'].std()\ndf['Age_of_Driver_norm'] = (df['Age_of_Driver'] - df['Age_of_Driver'].mean()) / df['Age_of_Driver'].std()\n# Usefulness: Normalization ensures features are scaled properly and avoids dominance of features with larger numeric ranges. Both engine size and driver age are likely correlated with driver behavior (potentially sex of the driver), and normalization preserves their relative differences for the downstream model.\n\n# Step 5: Casualty_Age_Gap\ndf['Casualty_Age_Gap'] = abs(df['Age_of_Driver'] - df['Age_of_Casualty'])\n# Feature description: Absolute difference between driver age and associated casualty age.\n# Usefulness: This feature may highlight younger drivers or older drivers with less cautious driving habits, which could correlate with sex of the driver.\n\n# Step 6: High_Casualty_Vehicles\ndf['High_Casualty_Vehicles'] = np.where(df['Number_of_Casualties'] > df['Number_of_Vehicles'], 1, 0)\n# Feature description: Binary indicator of cases where the number of casualties exceeds the number of vehicles involved in the accident.\n# Usefulness: Certain risky driving behaviors might be associated with the driver's sex. A higher ratio of casualties could align with reckless driving patterns or other risky characteristics of one gender.\nend", "Here are three potential next steps for feature engineering to improve predictions for the downstream task of predicting the sex of the driver (Sex_of_Driver):\n\n### Step 1: Compute Driver Casualty Age Ratio\npython\n# Feature name: Driver_Casualty_Age_Ratio\n# Feature description: This feature calculates the ratio of the age of the driver to the age of the casualty in each accident.\n# Usefulness: It provides insight into whether certain age relationships between drivers and casualties correlate with specific genders of drivers. Traditionally, certain demographics might exhibit patterns in age differences during accidents.\ndf['Driver_Casualty_Age_Ratio'] = df['Age_of_Driver'] / df['Age_of_Casualty']\nend\n\n### Step 2: Create Interaction between Speed Risk and Engine Capacity\npython\n# Feature name: Speed_Engine_Interaction\n# Feature description: This feature measures the interaction between Speed_Risk_Feature and normalized Engine_Capacity_(CC). It captures the combined contribution of vehicle speed risk and engine power.\n# Usefulness: Faster vehicles or those with larger engines might exhibit gender-specific trends in terms of the driver's tendency to take risks or cause casualties. Interaction features often enhance modeling performance by capturing non-linear relationships between variables.\ndf['Speed_Engine_Interaction'] = df['Speed_Risk_Feature'] * df['Engine_Capacity_(CC)_norm']\nend\n\n### Step 3: Group and Encode Age Bands\npython\n# Feature name: Age_Band_Target_Encoding\n# Feature description: This feature applies target encoding to the categorical Age_Band_of_Driver_bin based on the average Sex_of_Driver, revealing age-specific variations in driver gender.\n# Usefulness: Age bands can influence gender likelihood due to societal factors, driving habits, or insurance-related distributions. Encoding this feature using target value relationships helps provide a more meaningful representation for predictive tasks.\nage_band_mean = df.groupby('Age_Band_of_Driver_bin')['Sex_of_Driver'].mean()\ndf['Age_Band_Target_Encoding'] = df['Age_Band_of_Driver_bin'].map(age_band_mean)\nend\n\n### Dropping Irrelevant Columns\nHere are some columns that might be dropped for various reasons, such as being redundant, highly correlated with other features, or having little relevance to the prediction task:\n\n#### Example Column Dropping: \npython\n# Explanation why the column 2nd_Road_Number is dropped\n# The ‘2nd_Road_Number’ has a high incidence of zero values and seems unlikely to provide meaningful predictive information about the driver’s gender.\ndf.drop(columns=['2nd_Road_Number'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Casualty_Reference is dropped\n# ‘Casualty_Reference’ is merely an identifier for the casualties and does not contribute meaningful predictive insight into the driver's gender.\ndf.drop(columns=['Casualty_Reference'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n### Key Observations and Improvements:\n1. Driver-Centric Features: Focusing on interactions involving the driver (age, speed, vehicle type) can reveal behavioral and accident trends tied to gender.\n2. Target Encoding: Encoding categorical variables based on their relationship to the target (Sex_of_Driver) is a powerful technique to enhance predictive feature richness.\n3. Removing Noise: Columns with identifiers (e.g., 2nd_Road_Number) or other irrelevant features naturally reduce signal-to-noise ratios and can safely be dropped.\n\nEach step is carefully chosen to enrich the dataset with meaningful insights, reduce redundancy, and simplify downstream model learning complexities.", "python\n# Driver_Age_Speed_Interaction\n# Feature description: This feature interacts the normalized age of the driver (Age_of_Driver_norm) with the speed-risk feature (Speed_Risk_Feature). It captures how a driver’s age and presumed driving behavior combine to influence risk.\ndf['Driver_Age_Speed_Interaction'] = df['Age_of_Driver_norm'] * df['Speed_Risk_Feature']\n# Usefulness: Drivers of different age groups might react differently under high-speed situations, and hence, this interaction could capture behavior patterns that influence the likelihood of an accident and correlate with the driver's sex.\nend\n\npython\n# Vehicle_Energy_Risk\n# Feature description: This feature calculates the product of normalized engine capacity (Engine_Capacity_(CC)_norm) and speed risk (Speed_Risk_Feature) divided by vehicle age (Age_of_Vehicle). This represents the energy risk score for a vehicle.\ndf['Vehicle_Energy_Risk'] = (df['Engine_Capacity_(CC)_norm'] * df['Speed_Risk_Feature']) / (df['Age_of_Vehicle'] + 1)\n# Usefulness: Older vehicles might have lower safety standards in combination with the engine capacity and speed, potentially impacting driver behavior. Capturing this relationship may yield insights into identifying the driver's sex.\nend\n\npython\n# Urban_to_Rural_Interaction\n# Feature description: This feature interacts the Urban_or_Rural_Area variable with the Local_Authority_(District). It highlights differences in behavior or external environmental factors related to urban versus rural areas for different districts.\ndf['Urban_to_Rural_Interaction'] = df['Urban_or_Rural_Area'] * df['Local_Authority_(District)']\n# Usefulness: Driving conditions and patterns differ significantly between urban and rural areas. Combining city-type with local district identifiers may uncover spatial driving trends relevant to the sex of drivers.\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Driver_Casualty_Age_Ratio is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['Driver_Casualty_Age_Ratio'], inplace=True)\n# Reason: The Driver_Casualty_Age_Ratio feature has issues due to extreme values (e.g., infinity) and undefined standard deviation (NaN). This makes it unreliable for downstream predictive tasks and may negatively impact model generalization.\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 1st_Road_Number is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['1st_Road_Number'], inplace=True)\n# Reason: The road number, represented by a numerical feature, has no inherent relationship or pattern with the driver's sex. It's likely categorical or id-based and does not add predictive value to the classifier.\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column Hit_Object_in_Carriageway is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['Hit_Object_in_Carriageway'], inplace=True)\n# Reason: The Hit_Object_in_Carriageway feature shows a high concentration of zeros (Q3 = 0.0). It likely has low variance and limited impact on predicting the driver's sex, reducing its utility for feature extraction.\nend"), with improvements -
